But we also formally have many wonderful
resources on campus,

00:30:42.630 --> 00:30:45.550
which include both the computer forum.

00:30:45.550 --> 00:30:48.010
And we can go ahead and take a look at
their website.

00:30:48.010 --> 00:30:52.710
The computer forum is a great organization
here on campus.

00:30:52.710 --> 00:30:56.120
They are working specifically for students
that look to enter careers in

00:30:56.120 --> 00:30:58.460
Electrical Engineering and computer
science.

00:30:58.460 --> 00:31:03.910
They have over 60 members within Silicon
Valley and also worldwide,

00:31:03.910 --> 00:31:06.950
they hold career fairs, resume workshops,
mock interviews.

00:31:06.950 --> 00:31:08.870
They do site visits.

00:31:08.870 --> 00:31:10.400
Really great opportunity.

00:31:10.400 --> 00:31:14.540
And then, of course, we have Stanford's
Career Development Center.

00:31:14.540 --> 00:31:16.910
And this is really more for a general
audience.

00:31:16.910 --> 00:31:18.720
They have many of the traditional
resources.

00:31:18.720 --> 00:31:23.170
Again, the career fair, the mock
interviews resume workshops.

00:31:23.170 --> 00:31:28.128
But of course, this includes a very heavy
focus on CS and EE.

00:31:28.128 --> 00:31:30.783
And they have dozens of connections with
companies and

00:31:30.783 --> 00:31:33.677
opportunities here in Silicon Valley and
worldwide.

00:31:33.677 --> 00:31:37.270
So that's a bit of the perspective from
the EE side.

00:31:37.270 --> 00:31:39.630
Becky, do you want to talk about some of
the GSB side?

00:31:39.630 --> 00:31:40.130
&gt;&gt; Sure.
&gt;&gt; Thanks.

00:31:40.130 --> 00:31:45.770
Yes, so like Katy was saying, there's a
lot of opportunities at the GSB for

00:31:45.770 --> 00:31:48.940
one to one connections, networking, that
sort of thing.

00:31:48.940 --> 00:31:53.761
And we do a lot to facilitate that through
the Career Management Center.

00:31:53.761 --> 00:31:56.500
We start off in the fall for full-time.

00:31:56.500 --> 00:32:00.478
We have a very structured on-campus
recruiting process, so

00:32:00.478 --> 00:32:04.607
if you decide you want to pursue a career
at a large tech company,

00:32:04.607 --> 00:32:09.196
we have a large variety that come to
campus for on-campus recruiting,

00:32:09.196 --> 00:32:14.878
everything from Apple, Google, Microsoft,
Amazon, Cisco, Intuit, you name it.

00:32:14.878 --> 00:32:17.588
They're pretty much interacting with us.

00:32:17.588 --> 00:32:23.560
But we also have companies such as
Dropbox, Box Facebook, LinkedIn.

00:32:23.560 --> 00:32:26.961
These companies are coming to campus as
well for on-campus recruiting.

00:32:26.961 --> 00:32:31.413
We have an internship program with, with
simila, similar list of companies.

00:32:31.413 --> 00:32:35.236
Those companies come to recruit on campus
in the winter, so

00:32:35.236 --> 00:32:37.282
in January for internship.

00:32:37.282 --> 00:32:38.970
And that's our structured approach.

00:32:38.970 --> 00:32:43.210
We put on several company networking
nights, where these companies will come

00:32:43.210 --> 00:32:47.370
and basically, you know, mingle and
interact with students.

00:32:47.370 --> 00:32:50.084
And get to know them and understand and
students get to

00:32:50.084 --> 00:32:54.249
engage with those companies and understand
what opportunities both full time and

00:32:54.249 --> 00:32:57.940
internship-wise they can pursue with those
companies.

00:32:57.940 --> 00:33:02.490
In addition to that, we also host a number
of career treks.

00:33:02.490 --> 00:33:03.870
So we organize trips.

00:33:03.870 --> 00:33:05.280
One of them is called a Tech Crawl.

00:33:05.280 --> 00:33:09.490
So envision, we call them, you know, for
those of you in the US, you may be

00:33:09.490 --> 00:33:14.170
familiar with a pub crawl or a progressive
style dinner where students will be on

00:33:14.170 --> 00:33:19.390
a bus and go from company to company and
meet at their offices in San Francisco.

00:33:19.390 --> 00:33:22.632
Student clubs also organize similar treks
so

00:33:22.632 --> 00:33:28.431
our Entrepreneurship Club as well as our
High Tech Club and a number of others will

00:33:28.431 --> 00:33:34.254
also organize treks to companies around
the Bay Area as well as other locations.

00:33:34.254 --> 00:33:38.505
So in the last two years, students have
organized a trek to Austin for

00:33:38.505 --> 00:33:39.838
South by Southwest.

00:33:39.838 --> 00:33:43.688
Where there's basically thousands of
companies to meet.

00:33:43.688 --> 00:33:47.179
And the CMC, the Career Management Center,

00:33:47.179 --> 00:33:53.155
also hosts an event called fewer than 300
or FT3, as we have abbreviated it.

00:33:53.155 --> 00:33:57.552
In which we invite specifically companies,
about 30 companies,

00:33:57.552 --> 00:34:01.189
which students have indicated of interest
to kind of

00:34:01.189 --> 00:34:04.857
have a funky Silicon Valley-Stanford GSB
field event.

00:34:04.857 --> 00:34:09.750
So we've had some pretty amazing startups
come to that event.

00:34:09.750 --> 00:34:12.860
And we've had some pretty amazing
decorations and, and fun

00:34:12.860 --> 00:34:16.350
interactions where students have actually
sourced full time jobs and internships.

00:34:16.350 --> 00:34:20.490
So there's a whole slew of opportunities
with which to engage with companies.

00:34:20.490 --> 00:34:24.780
&gt;&gt; Now for joint degree students who would
have two summers between their three

00:34:24.780 --> 00:34:28.920
years, would they then be able to do a
summer internship again?

00:34:28.920 --> 00:34:29.870
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, yeah, yes.

00:34:31.000 --> 00:34:32.506
Well I guess from the GSP side, yes.

00:34:32.506 --> 00:34:35.995
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Absolutely, I mean our students
are, they are very much encouraged,

00:34:35.995 --> 00:34:39.590
right from the get go, to start planning
for that summer internship after their

00:34:39.590 --> 00:34:42.781
first year and it would absolutely be the
case after that second year.

00:34:42.781 --> 00:34:45.471
And I think Becky really, you know hit
the,

00:34:45.471 --> 00:34:50.200
the point, you know, well that the
companies are coming here to Stanford and

00:34:50.200 --> 00:34:54.203
our, the Stanford students really have a
great reputation rep,

00:34:54.203 --> 00:34:59.480
representation out there in the sense that
you know, they're great students.

00:34:59.480 --> 00:35:02.850
And these companies are really coming to
find our students.

00:35:02.850 --> 00:35:04.490
They're seeking our students out.

00:35:04.490 --> 00:35:07.830
And our students aren't going out there
and seeking out these internships.

00:35:07.830 --> 00:35:10.040
But really, a lot of the opportunities are
coming to them.

00:35:10.040 --> 00:35:12.800
I think that was something for me that was
really interesting to

00:35:12.800 --> 00:35:16.590
see was to see these big companies, like
Google and Apple, showing up and

00:35:16.590 --> 00:35:20.840
really sort of wining and dining our
students to see them for internships.

00:35:20.840 --> 00:35:24.380
Another which often turns into those
full-time positions.

00:35:24.380 --> 00:35:25.070
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:35:25.070 --> 00:35:27.839
Yeah, I think there are many opportunities
in,

00:35:27.839 --> 00:35:31.212
in a way, the challenge is to figure out
what you wanna do and

00:35:31.212 --> 00:35:35.665
I think our career center does a great job
of helping you figure out what it is you

00:35:35.665 --> 00:35:40.150
wanna do, not just in terms of your job
but how you're job fits into your life.

00:35:40.150 --> 00:35:40.974
Would you say [CROSSTALK].

00:35:40.974 --> 00:35:42.570
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, yes.

00:35:42.570 --> 00:35:45.160
So we have a number of pro, a number of
programs.

00:35:45.160 --> 00:35:46.990
Two in particular, one is called Career
Design.

00:35:46.990 --> 00:35:49.970
Which first-year students at the GSB will
pursue.

00:35:49.970 --> 00:35:54.200
It is basically it takes place in the
first six weeks of your time as

00:35:54.200 --> 00:35:56.583
a first-year student here at the GSB.

00:35:56.583 --> 00:36:00.271
And it basically allows you to take some
time to think about what it is that you're

00:36:00.271 --> 00:36:04.013
interested in, what you're passionate
about, you know, what gets you going,

00:36:04.013 --> 00:36:07.334
what do you want to do from a short term
and a long term perspective.

00:36:07.334 --> 00:36:10.342
We have a program also called Career and
Life Vision, or

00:36:10.342 --> 00:36:12.770
CLV, and this has a similar approach.

00:36:12.770 --> 00:36:15.870
It's kind of a, a program within the
program.

00:36:15.870 --> 00:36:19.710
That allows you to not only engage with
our with our career advisors who can help,

00:36:19.710 --> 00:36:21.990
kind of, help you plan out your career and
life vision.

00:36:21.990 --> 00:36:24.930
But, let's say you, you think you know
what you want to do but

00:36:24.930 --> 00:36:29.000
you really need someone to talk through
that process with we have plenty of

00:36:29.000 --> 00:36:32.330
people that are wonderful support staff to
help you with that.

00:36:32.330 --> 00:36:34.308
So, there's a lot of opportunities to
explore.

00:36:34.308 --> 00:36:38.370
&gt;&gt; And the Career Center is not only
helping you figure out what you want to

00:36:38.370 --> 00:36:39.430
do, but prepare for

00:36:39.430 --> 00:36:44.460
your job search, and interviewing skills,
all the way to when you get an offer, and

00:36:44.460 --> 00:36:48.480
how to negotiate that salary all the way
to the end of, of the process.

00:36:49.590 --> 00:36:54.470
In terms of social life for joint and dual
degree students as I, as I mentioned,

00:36:54.470 --> 00:36:59.410
we have several other joint and dual
degree programs here so we do have a lot

00:36:59.410 --> 00:37:04.430
of experience with Mary in particular with
working with joint degree students.

00:37:04.430 --> 00:37:06.330
So Mary can you comment on,

00:37:06.330 --> 00:37:09.990
what it is like for a joint degree student
in terms of social life?

00:37:09.990 --> 00:37:12.890
Do they sort of feel like they're, you
know,

00:37:12.890 --> 00:37:15.270
affiliated more with one school or
another?

00:37:15.270 --> 00:37:15.770
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:37:15.770 --> 00:37:16.670
That's a great question.

00:37:16.670 --> 00:37:19.908
I think, first of all, it depends on what
program you start with.

00:37:19.908 --> 00:37:24.027
And that you're going to build
relationships with the cohorts that

00:37:24.027 --> 00:37:25.481
you're connected to.

00:37:25.481 --> 00:37:26.855
So if you start at the GSB,

00:37:26.855 --> 00:37:29.927
I think your going to feel most connected
to MBA students.

00:37:29.927 --> 00:37:33.567
If you start in Electrical Engineering,
you're first going to build up kind of

00:37:33.567 --> 00:37:37.103
your sense of community with those
Electrical Engineering students.

00:37:37.103 --> 00:37:39.734
But what I have seen happen in other
programs is as

00:37:39.734 --> 00:37:44.020
students start progressing through and
doing blended quarters.

00:37:44.020 --> 00:37:48.480
They start to build up kind of the social
relationships in the other program.

00:37:48.480 --> 00:37:52.208
And then they have what I like to call the
first world problem of having to

00:37:52.208 --> 00:37:55.340
balance [LAUGH] the relationship that both
schools and

00:37:55.340 --> 00:37:59.980
then all of the events and activities,
that're happening all across Stanford.

00:37:59.980 --> 00:38:03.513
We're certainly not at a loss of things to
do, events to go to,

00:38:03.513 --> 00:38:06.490
food to eat, music to hear [LAUGH].

00:38:06.490 --> 00:38:09.280
Both here at Stanford and just in general
in the Bay Area.

00:38:09.280 --> 00:38:12.660
So I think students find really rich
social life.

00:38:12.660 --> 00:38:15.110
And sometimes you know,

00:38:15.110 --> 00:38:19.020
part of the draw for wanting to stay for
an extra year is being able to

00:38:19.020 --> 00:38:23.490
take advantage of everything that the Bay
Area and Stanford University has to offer.

00:38:24.660 --> 00:38:29.490
Oh, and I also wanna add once you are
enrolled in a Joint Degree Program.

00:38:29.490 --> 00:38:31.960
Let's say you start at Electrical
Engineering, but

00:38:31.960 --> 00:38:34.140
you've been accepted to the MBA program.

00:38:34.140 --> 00:38:38.040
You do have access to GSB student
activities and events.

00:38:38.040 --> 00:38:40.010
And so you are very welcome to come here
and

00:38:40.010 --> 00:38:45.660
to sign up for clubs and to attend
lectures, activities whatever you'd like.

00:38:45.660 --> 00:38:50.040
So really the limitations are personal of

00:38:50.040 --> 00:38:55.280
what you feel comfortable with pursuing or
not pursuing at your first year here.

00:38:55.280 --> 00:38:57.430
&gt;&gt; And Katy are, are extra curricular and

00:38:57.430 --> 00:39:02.110
clubs at Engineering opened to Business
School students in the same way?

00:39:02.110 --> 00:39:02.860
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.
I mean I

00:39:02.860 --> 00:39:05.370
think it depends on what you're most
interested in.

00:39:05.370 --> 00:39:08.820
But we have a number of different clubs
that are specific to the research

00:39:08.820 --> 00:39:10.580
areas within the EE.

00:39:10.580 --> 00:39:14.215
We also have a group called the Graduate
Students in Electrical Engineering and

00:39:14.215 --> 00:39:17.143
I know that they would more than welcome
students outside of

00:39:17.143 --> 00:39:18.407
Electrical Engineering.

00:39:18.407 --> 00:39:22.262
They do happy hours and they do workshops
with our faculty.

00:39:22.262 --> 00:39:27.622
So students that are really interested in
getting engaged within EE would certain be

00:39:27.622 --> 00:39:29.420
more than welcome to join us.

00:39:29.420 --> 00:39:30.630
&gt;&gt; All right.

00:39:30.630 --> 00:39:33.990
We only have a couple of minutes left, and
so I'm going to ask each of

00:39:33.990 --> 00:39:39.150
you if you have any advice for our
prospective applicants out there,

00:39:39.150 --> 00:39:43.590
either about preparing for the program, or
once they're a student here.

00:39:43.590 --> 00:39:45.660
Katy?
&gt;&gt; Sure so I think I,

00:39:45.660 --> 00:39:48.576
I could say I have two little pieces of
advice.

00:39:48.576 --> 00:39:50.774
One would be to start planning early and

00:39:50.774 --> 00:39:53.577
to get organized early on, if that's
possible.

00:39:53.577 --> 00:39:56.828
And then my second piece of advice is
something that I actually recommend to

00:39:56.828 --> 00:39:59.474
anyone that's applying to graduate
graduate school.

00:39:59.474 --> 00:40:05.261
And that's to do informational interviews
with people that are in your dream job,

00:40:05.261 --> 00:40:08.567
your dream field your dream career path
and

00:40:08.567 --> 00:40:13.707
find out how they got to that position,
what degrees they have.

00:40:13.707 --> 00:40:17.802
Also take a look at what they would be
looking at in terms of hiring somebody for

00:40:17.802 --> 00:40:18.874
that position now.

00:40:18.874 --> 00:40:23.424
Just to find out exactly what it is you do
need do you need the dual degree,

00:40:23.424 --> 00:40:28.250
do you need the MBA, do you need a PhD or
do you need an MS degree?

00:40:28.250 --> 00:40:31.180
And that again, is something that I would
recommend to anyone applying to

00:40:31.180 --> 00:40:31.900
graduate school so

00:40:31.900 --> 00:40:36.840
that you really hone in on what it is that
you need to achieve your career goals.

00:40:37.970 --> 00:40:38.580
&gt;&gt; Great, Mary?

00:40:39.600 --> 00:40:41.740
&gt;&gt; Well, I think Katy already said it
really well, but

00:40:41.740 --> 00:40:45.990
what I would add is that I think there's a
lot of exciting options at Stanford, and

00:40:45.990 --> 00:40:48.190
it's easy to get overwhelmed.

00:40:48.190 --> 00:40:52.780
And so, any work you can do to get clear
on where you wanna go,

00:40:52.780 --> 00:40:56.230
what you wanna do using some of the
strategies that Katy's

00:40:56.230 --> 00:40:59.770
already mentioned I think are gonna come
in really handy.

00:40:59.770 --> 00:41:03.690
I find that students who come in with a
vision, and that vision may change.

00:41:03.690 --> 00:41:06.550
But hence, they have a vision, I think
they get more out of

00:41:06.550 --> 00:41:10.580
their overall experience, because they
already have a sense of focus of what they

00:41:10.580 --> 00:41:14.770
wanna say yes to and equally important,
what they should be saying no to.

00:41:14.770 --> 00:41:15.890
Because there's just so

00:41:15.890 --> 00:41:20.450
many things that you could be doing to
support your education.

00:41:20.450 --> 00:41:23.670
So I would just say take that time to
really try to get clear,

00:41:23.670 --> 00:41:27.370
even if it's a general vision, but to have
some type of vision.

00:41:27.370 --> 00:41:30.830
Because the programs don't necessarily
help you find a vision.

00:41:30.830 --> 00:41:32.560
The programs help you get to your vision.

00:41:32.560 --> 00:41:34.450
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:41:34.450 --> 00:41:35.001
Becky?

00:41:35.001 --> 00:41:37.403
&gt;&gt; So I, I totally agree with what Mary
and Katy had said.

00:41:37.403 --> 00:41:40.597
I was actually gonna talk about the
informational interviews with your dream,

00:41:40.597 --> 00:41:42.100
people in your dream job as well.

00:41:42.100 --> 00:41:44.480
So I, I'll get a little more tactical on
that front.

00:41:44.480 --> 00:41:48.280
So this may seem you know, obvious to some
of you and for others it

00:41:48.280 --> 00:41:52.670
maybe seem a little daunting, but I would
say in order to identify those people in

00:41:52.670 --> 00:41:57.280
your dream job roles if you haven't done
so already, take advantage of LinkedIn.

00:41:57.280 --> 00:42:00.590
You know, go ahead and plug in a company
where you want to work or

00:42:00.590 --> 00:42:03.280
a function that you want to pursue at
those companies.

00:42:03.280 --> 00:42:07.280
You can go into your advanced search in
Linkedn and just plug those things in and

00:42:07.280 --> 00:42:08.410
see who comes up.

00:42:08.410 --> 00:42:12.570
You can plug it in by region, so if your
specific to a location, you can add that,

00:42:12.570 --> 00:42:17.070
and obviously it's, it's great to have
these meetings in person, but if you

00:42:17.070 --> 00:42:22.210
can't, you know, Google Hangouts or Skype
even just 15, 20 minutes of someones time.

00:42:22.210 --> 00:42:25.655
Be sure when you go into those
informationals, if it's over the phone or

00:42:25.655 --> 00:42:28.910
in-person, have questions prepared in
advance,

00:42:28.910 --> 00:42:30.820
be really thoughtful about what you want
to ask.

00:42:30.820 --> 00:42:34.800
The worst thing that we see is when
students' asks for

00:42:34.800 --> 00:42:36.580
informationals, a great contact, and they
show up,

00:42:36.580 --> 00:42:38.830
and they haven't prepared or they haven't
done any research.

00:42:38.830 --> 00:42:40.540
So, we know none of you will do that,

00:42:40.540 --> 00:42:44.190
but make sure you know you have those
questions ready to go.

00:42:44.190 --> 00:42:47.380
Keep your requests short and sweet, you
know, one to two,

00:42:47.380 --> 00:42:51.960
max three sentences about what you wanna
ask for and then send a thank you.

00:42:51.960 --> 00:42:55.820
So if you engage with these people who
have given you their time be sure to

00:42:55.820 --> 00:42:56.810
send a follow-up thank you note,

00:42:56.810 --> 00:43:00.760
even if it's just three sentences max
that, you know, wrapping up,

00:43:00.760 --> 00:43:03.930
filling them in on where you're at in your
process, they'll really appreciate that.

00:43:05.140 --> 00:43:06.420
&gt;&gt; Jack.

00:43:06.420 --> 00:43:08.080
&gt;&gt; Think for financial aid perspective,

00:43:08.080 --> 00:43:14.200
just, you can figure it out after you've
decided what's the best pursuit,

00:43:14.200 --> 00:43:19.270
whether it's just an MBA, just an MS in
EE, or doing the joint degree.

00:43:19.270 --> 00:43:24.060
&gt;&gt; And if we haven't made it clear earlier
you can apply to these

00:43:25.570 --> 00:43:28.690
before you arrived for the Joint Degree
Program.

00:43:28.690 --> 00:43:32.540
But you could if you only wanted to apply
for the MBA.

00:43:32.540 --> 00:43:36.113
You could apply to the Engineering school
as a first year MBA student.

00:43:36.113 --> 00:43:41.111
Or if you end up starting in Engineering,
you could apply for

00:43:41.111 --> 00:43:45.029
the MBA and add it as a first year
Engineering student.

00:43:45.029 --> 00:43:51.570
So you, you do have that chance to to take
some time to think about it.

00:43:52.700 --> 00:43:56.910
Well I want to thank all of you for your
time this afternoon,

00:43:56.910 --> 00:44:00.160
I hope that it's been helpful to learn
about the program,

00:44:00.160 --> 00:44:04.180
and whatever you decide, I wish you all
the best.

